{"qid":"Q3167772","names":["Kingdom of Polonnaruwa"],"title":"Kingdom of Polonnaruwa","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Kingdom of Polonnaruwa was the second great mediaeval Sinhalese state, established when Vijayabahu I expelled the Chola occupiers from the island by 1070 and made Polonnaruwa his capital. Its apex came under Parakramabahu I (r. 1153–1186), who united the island under a single throne for the last time in antiquity, launched naval expeditions to southern India and Burma, and constructed 1,470 irrigation reservoirs including the vast Parakrama Samudra sea. After Nissanka Malla (r. 1187–1196) the throne fractured through dynastic feuds and South Indian invasions; in 1215 Kalinga Magha invaded from Kalinga with a coalition army, sacked the capital, and drove the Sinhalese population southward, ending Polonnaruwa's centrality and prompting the foundation of the Kingdom of Dambadeniya in 1232.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Polonnaruwa's territory covering most of Sri Lanka at its peak is well-attested in Sinhalese chronicles (Mahavamsa, Culavamsa) and the archaeological record; the precise fluctuation of frontier zones with South Indian polities is less certain.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Kingdom of Polonnaruwa","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_Polonnaruwa"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Parakramabahu I","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parakramabahu_I"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Kalinga Magha","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalinga_Magha"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Vijayabahu I","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vijayabahu_I"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wi